  <section class="stack-lg">
    <p class="eyebrow">About the framework</p>
    <h1>
      Ethotechnics: governance and care infrastructure for high-stakes systems
    </h1>
    <p class="section-lead">
      Ethotechnics is the engineering discipline concerned with keeping
      authority, evidence, capability, consequence, and correction coupled
      tightly enough that increasing machine agency does not silently become
      unreviewable institutional power. It treats evidence, authorization,
      challenge, reconsideration, correction, and escalation as explicit system
      states, properties a system either has or lacks, rather than policy
      documents around the system. The
      <a href="/method">method</a> states the chain, the state variables, and the
      laws.
    </p>
    <p>
      Stated plainly: the practice of designing technology so that technical
      power remains bounded, contestable, reversible, and answerable for the
      burdens it creates.
    </p>
    <p>
      The framework is authored by <strong>Kanav Jain</strong>, and developed
      through proposed standards, glossary curation, field diagnostics, and
      implementation-oriented research. Its standards are proposed, not imposed:
      each carries an editorial status (Draft or Stable), and adoption by
      institutions is what makes one authoritative.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">Institute and Studio</p>
    <h2>How the Institute and Studio fit together</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Institute</strong> is the public, open-access commons:
      standards, ontology, glossary, diagnostics, and field notes are free to use,
      cite, and extend.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Ethotechnics Studio</strong> provides clinical AI safety evaluations,
      VC diligence, and governance advisory through commissioned engagements. The
      Institute publishes the open framework; the Studio applies it to specific systems
      and organizations.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        Visit Ethotechnics Studio →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What existing standards don't cover</p>
    <h2>The gap Ethotechnics addresses</h2>
    <p>
      NIST AI RMF tells you to manage risk. EU AI Act tells you to document
      compliance. ISO/IEC 42001 tells you to build a management system. OECD AI
      Principles tell you to be responsible. None of them tell you whether your
      system can actually be stopped when it's harming someone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Existing frameworks govern by representation: documents, roles, reviews,
      and attestations. Ethotechnics governs by control: stop rules, binding
      clocks, rollback authority, and contestability that changes state.
    </p>
    <table class="mapping-table mapping-table--compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Existing standard says</th>
          <th scope="col">Ethotechnics requires</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says"
            >"Maintain human oversight" <span class="small muted"
              >(EU AI Act, Art. 14)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires"
            >Named human with stop authority, tested halt path, recovery clock</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says"
            >"Manage risks across the AI lifecycle" <span class="small muted"
              >(NIST AI RMF)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires"
            >Measurable time-in-harm bounds, exercised rollback paths</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says"
            >"Conduct conformity assessment" <span class="small muted"
              >(ISO/IEC 42001)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires"
            >Evidence the system can be stopped mid-incident, not just
            documented as compliant</td
          >
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Existing standard says"
            >"Implement responsible AI principles" <span class="small muted"
              >(OECD)</span
            ></td
          >
          <td data-label="Ethotechnics requires"
            >Binding escalation: owner + timer + action, or the system degrades</td
          >
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted">
      Ethotechnics does not compete with these standards on whether governance
      is "important." It addresses what they leave undertreated:
      <a href="/standards/#comparison">see the full comparison →</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      The differentiation is not only against standards but against four nearby
      fields. AI ethics asks whether systems are fair, transparent, private, or
      aligned. Safety engineering asks whether they malfunction.
      Human-centered design asks whether they work for their users.
      Governance asks who oversees them. Ethotechnics cuts across all four and
      asks something prior: how should technical systems be built so that
      increases in capability do not automatically become increases in
      domination? A system can be accurate, transparent, human-supervised,
      democratically authorized, and socially beneficial, and still be bad —
      if its success depends on hidden burden transfer, unusable appeals, or
      authority expanded beyond what was justified. What the discipline
      considers low-leverage is catalogued at{" "}
      <a href="/low-leverage">low-leverage work</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The governance landscape</p>
    <h2>Where Ethotechnics fits</h2>
    <p>
      AI governance is a rapidly growing domain. Enterprises invest in
      compliance platforms, evaluation tools, and guardrail systems to meet
      emerging regulatory requirements.
    </p>
    <p>
      Ethotechnics is not a product. It publishes benchmarks and test cases that
      governance platforms can be evaluated against — the role NIST frameworks
      play for compliance tools, or OWASP standards for security tools. Those
      bodies hold that role because institutions adopted them. Ethotechnics is
      proposed for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Institute provides the open standards. The Studio provides
      <a
        href="https://ethotechnics.com"
        rel="noopener noreferrer"
        target="_blank"
      >
        evaluations and diligence →
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">What it is for</p>
    <h2>Reliance without subordination</h2>
    <p>
      Institutions already rely on things they cannot fully inspect: colleagues,
      suppliers, instruments, the last shift's notes. That reliance is what
      makes an institution more than one person, and nothing here argues against
      it. What the framework is for is keeping reliance on a system from turning
      into subordination to it, in either direction. The institution should be
      able to depend on a system without losing the capacity to question,
      narrow, or replace it. The people the system's decisions land on should be
      able to depend on the institution without losing the standing to object
      and be answered.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every law on the site is a constraint that keeps one of those two
      dependences from converting into command. Capability does not become
      authority; a quiet year does not become renewal; a captive population does
      not become a population that may be directed. The
      <a href="/standards/laws">laws</a> state the constraints, the
      <a href="/standards/core-axioms">axioms</a> state what they are owed to, and
      the <a href="/research/theory">theory essays</a> say why. A reader can adopt
      the constraints without the ideal. The ideal is what they add up to.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The compact ambition: systems that can keep becoming more capable
      without becoming more entitled to rule.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">The underlying project</p>
    <h2>Legitimate power under permanent institutional fallibility</h2>
    <p>
      Strip the technology away and the underlying claim concerns the
      relationship among knowledge, power, dependence, burden, and correction.
      The question it answers: what kinds of power does a technical system
      create, who bears its failures, and what prevents that power from
      becoming final? The chain behind it is short. Institutions must
      represent reality to act. Representation is necessarily incomplete.
      Incompleteness creates residual burdens. Those burdens are distributed
      politically. Power becomes dominating when it externalizes them while
      insulating itself from correction.
    </p>
    <p>
      Domination, in this frame, is power insulated from{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/consequential-contradiction"
        >consequential contradiction</a
      >: the institution defines the relevant reality, acts on its definition,
      pushes the residual downward, controls what counts as evidence against
      itself, and cannot be forced to revise by those it gets wrong. The
      epistemology is fallibilist rather than skeptical: knowledge stays
      defeasible because <a href="/glossary/excluded-case">the excluded
      case</a> is potentially evidence about the category, and that
      fallibilism is material — a system is not meaningfully open to
      correction if the people who know it is wrong must destroy themselves to
      prove it.
    </p>
    <p>
      No arrangement possesses constitutive innocence: markets, states,
      democratic majorities, expertise, participation, and emancipatory goals
      all face the same permanent question — whatever arrangement you favor,
      what happens when it gets someone wrong? The five concerns of the design
      discipline follow from that, and each is a working part of this site:
      jurisdiction (<a href="/glossary/non-conversion-principle">what the
      institution may decide at all</a>), representation (<a
        href="/glossary/safe-incompleteness">what it models and how that can
      be contested</a>), residual custody (<a
        href="/glossary/error-bearing-party">who carries what it cannot
      represent</a>), corrigibility (<a href="/glossary/exception-learning"
        >whether error travels back into the rule</a
      >), and non-finality (<a href="/glossary/non-finality">whether its
      decisions stay reopenable</a>).
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The compact form of the whole project: how can institutions be powerful
      enough to act without becoming powerful enough to define away the
      evidence that they are wrong?
    </p>
  </section>

