  <section class="stack-sm card">
    <p class="eyebrow">For academic readers</p>
    <h2>How to place this work</h2>
    <p>
      In the most general academic terms, the project is a{" "}
      <strong>fallibilist theory of institutional legitimacy</strong>: a theory
      of legitimacy built around the fact that institutions must act under
      incomplete knowledge, imperfect representation, and recurrent error. In
      political-theory vocabulary it is a republican theory of non-domination
      for epistemically fallible institutions, with the distinctive move that
      domination is explained partly in epistemic and administrative terms: an
      institution dominates when it controls the categories, evidentiary
      standards, and feedback mechanisms through which its own mistakes could
      become visible.
    </p>
    <p>
      Neighbouring formulations: a political epistemology of institutional
      representation, error, and correction; a constitutional theory of
      sociotechnical and administrative power (constitutional meaning the
      design of limits, jurisdictions, standing, review, and reversibility,
      not constitutional law); and, drawing on pragmatism, a materialist
      pragmatism of institutional correction — inquiry depends not on
      collective learning alone but on whether those bearing institutional
      failure have the standing and power to force reconsideration.
    </p>
    <p>
      The distinctive connection, more than any component concept, runs:
      representational exclusion → material externalization → asymmetric
      burden → blocked correction. In established vocabularies the components
      read as boundary work and invisible labor (STS), cost externalization
      and burden shifting (political economy), street-level bureaucracy and
      administrative burden (administrative studies), and epistemic exclusion
      with self-confirming institutional knowledge (social epistemology).
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      One sentence an academic can place: the project develops a fallibilist,
      non-domination-based theory of institutional legitimacy in which
      authority is justified only insofar as institutions remain contestable,
      corrigible, jurisdictionally bounded, and answerable for the burdens
      produced by their necessarily partial representations. Synthetically: a
      theory of corrigible institutions.
    </p>
  </section>

